### Method 1: Using the `log` crate

First, add the `log` crate to your `Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[dependencies]
log = "0.4"
env_logger = "0.9"
```

Then, you can configure the log level at runtime. For example, you can switch off all debug information by setting the log level to `info`, `warn`, `error`, etc.

Here's an example:

```rust
use log::{LevelFilter, info, warn};
use env_logger;

fn main() {
    // Initialize logging with a specific filter (e.g., info level)
    env_logger::builder().filter_level(LevelFilter::Info).init();

    // Example of debug and info messages
    info!("This is an info message");
    warn!("This is a warning message");

    // You can also change the log level dynamically
    if should_disable_debug() {
        info!("Disabling debug messages...");
        env_logger::Builder::from_env(env_logger::Env::default().default_filter_or("info")).init();
    }

    // Now, only info and higher level messages will be logged
    info!("After disabling debug, this message will still be logged.");
}
```

In this example, we initialize the logger with an info level filter. If `should_disable_debug()` returns `true`, we change the filter to `info` which will disable debug messages.

### Method 2: Directly Managing Output

If you prefer not to use the `log` crate, you can manage the output of debug information directly in your code. This approach involves checking a configuration flag or environment variable.

